  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match hypothyroidism PRS candidates are present. PGS005218 is best supported overall because it is explicitly trained/validated for incident hypothyroidism in a disease-focused GWAS/PRS study (Nat Genet, 2025) with very large training (n=1,146,562) and validation (n=441,692) and a genome-wide PRS-CS construction (~1.11M variants). However, its reported AUROC=0.859 is a full-model metric and the covariates include thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the provided domain_knowledge flags as near-outcome covariates that can severely inflate AUROC and are not comparable evidence of standalone PRS quality. Competing models (e.g., PGS000965/PGS001181) report PRS-only AUROC/R2 in UKB with cleaner covariate comparability but come from a pan-trait framework and have lower discrimination, while several other direct-match candidates either target therapy-induced hypothyroidism or lack PRS-comparable metrics. This mix of strong endpoint/study support but covariate-inflated performance reporting keeps the recommendation sub-optimal rather than high-confidence."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target concept of nodular goiter, including (i) nontoxic multinodular goiter scores (PGS001814, PGS002022) and (ii) benign nodular goiter scores (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273–PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-faithful for generic nodular goiter risk (their primary task is discrimination between cancer and benign nodular goiter, not nodular goiter status).

Among the endpoint-faithful benign nodular goiter options, PGS005262 is preferred because it uses a methodologically strong genome-wide continuous shrinkage approach (PRSCS) with very high variant count (~1.09M) and shows the strongest reported discrimination within that sibling set (full-model AUROC 0.5933) along with a relatively strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.41). By contrast, PGS005261 is P+T with only 110 variants and lower AUROC, and PGS005263 (also PRSCS) has slightly weaker AUROC and OR.

This is graded as sub-optimal rather than high-quality because key comparability information is missing/unclear for the leading candidates (covariates are ‘Unknown’ for the benign nodular goiter models, and the available AUROCs appear to be full-model rather than PRS-only), limiting confidence in cross-model ranking versus the UKB portability multinodular goiter scores, which report only weak PRS-comparable partial correlations."}
